From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 20 Sep 2026 23:58:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Application: show the busy dialog from the moment play is
 triggered

Kodi already raises a busy dialog while a file opens - GUI_MSG_PLAYBACK_STARTED
resets m_playerEvent and waits on it, OnAVStart sets it - but that begins once
a player exists, and a good deal happens before one does.

CApplication::PlayFile first calls GatherPlaybackDetails, which resolves the
item and then opens the video database and reads from it: LoadVideoInfo, the
resume point, the episode bookmark. With the library on another machine that is
a MySQL connect and several queries over the network, on the app thread, with
nothing on screen. Kodi sets MYSQL_OPT_CONNECT_TIMEOUT and no read timeout, so
those reads are unbounded.

What that looks like is a press that does nothing, with the stock wheel turning
up seconds later.

Cover that stretch too. It will not animate - the work after it is on this
thread - and that is the accepted trade here: the point is to acknowledge the
press at once. Kodi's own wheel takes over and animates once the player exists.

- CGUIDialogBusyNoCancel, not CGUIDialogBusy: the latter inherits privately and
  only CGUIWindowManager may open it. Both use DialogBusy.xml. CPowerManager
  already drives this dialog the same way.
- Its Open_Internal forces bProcessRenderLoop off, so Open() shows and returns
  rather than pumping until close.
- One Render() so it reaches the screen before the blocking work starts.
  CGUIWindowManager::ProcessRenderLoop would be the usual way and is private to
  CGUIDialog/CGUIMediaWindow, so this calls our own Render instead. Whether one
  Render is enough for it to appear is the part to confirm on hardware.
- Scoped, so every path out of PlayFile closes it, including the early returns
  for a playback error, a disc stub and an unselected playlist. A spinner left
  on screen is the failure worth designing against.

PlayFile is where a GUI click and a JSON-RPC Player.Open both arrive, so one
place covers both.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>

diff --git a/xbmc/application/Application.cpp b/xbmc/application/Application.cpp
index 8f4e7c2..c67a70c 100644
--- a/xbmc/application/Application.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/application/Application.cpp
@@ -76,6 +76,7 @@
 #include "guilib/GUIComponent.h"
 #include "guilib/GUIControlProfiler.h"
 #include "guilib/GUIFontManager.h"
+#include "dialogs/GUIDialogBusyNoCancel.h"
 #include "guilib/GUIWindowManager.h"
 #include "guilib/StereoscopicsManager.h"
 #include "guilib/TextureManager.h"
@@ -2074,8 +2075,48 @@ bool CApplication::PlayMedia(CFileItem& item, const std::string& player, PLAYLIS
   return PlayFile(item, player, false);
 }
 
+namespace
+{
+// Holds the busy dialog up for a scope. Open() on this one does not pump the
+// render loop - CGUIDialogBusyNoCancel::Open_Internal forces that off - so it
+// shows and returns, which is what is wanted here.
+class CPlayStartBusy
+{
+public:
+  CPlayStartBusy()
+  {
+    auto* dialog = CServiceBroker::GetGUI()
+                       ->GetWindowManager()
+                       .GetWindow<CGUIDialogBusyNoCancel>(WINDOW_DIALOG_BUSY_NOCANCEL);
+    if (dialog && !dialog->IsDialogRunning())
+    {
+      dialog->Open();
+      m_dialog = dialog;
+    }
+  }
+  ~CPlayStartBusy()
+  {
+    if (m_dialog)
+      m_dialog->Close(true);
+  }
+  bool IsShowing() const { return m_dialog != nullptr; }
+
+  CPlayStartBusy(const CPlayStartBusy&) = delete;
+  CPlayStartBusy& operator=(const CPlayStartBusy&) = delete;
+
+private:
+  CGUIDialogBusyNoCancel* m_dialog{nullptr};
+};
+} // namespace
+
 bool CApplication::PlayFile(CFileItem item, const std::string& player, bool bRestart /* = false */)
 {
+  // Closes on every path out of PlayFile, so a failure here cannot leave a
+  // spinner behind.
+  const CPlayStartBusy playStartBusy;
+  if (playStartBusy.IsShowing())
+    Render(); // draw it now; what follows blocks this thread
+
   const auto appPlayer{GetComponent<CApplicationPlayer>()};
   const auto stackHelper{GetComponent<CApplicationStackHelper>()};
 
